        "type": "content"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Remember that in most main-group element compounds, the valence electrons of the isolated atoms combine to form chemical bonds that satisfy the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "octet rule"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The tendency of main-group elements to form structures in which each atom has 8 valence electrons."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "548046493",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "482128945",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ". For example, the four valence electrons of carbon overlap with electrons from four hydrogen atoms to form a CH"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": " molecule, while the one valence electron leaves sodium and adds to the seven valence electrons of chlorine to form the ionic formula unit NaCl (see figure below)."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2810986526",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Covalent bonds involve the sharing of electrons "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(left)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " and ionic bonds involve the transferring of electrons associated with each bonding atom "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(right)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": ", as indicated by the colored electrons."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "629082997",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "3yonde5brznx1ul",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "display": "block",
            "height": 168,
            "id": "2838575502",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/14/142C1AE28338DA27C295D4E5E302D544/ch4_nacl_bonding.png",
            "type": "img",
            "width": 300
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "2533808164",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "editor": "slate",
        "id": "528228033",
        "textDirection": "ltr",
        "type": "content"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Transition metals do not normally bond in this fashion, because the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals are not limited by the octet rule. Instead, they primarily form coordinate covalent bonds through Lewis acid–base interactions. Both electrons in the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "coordinate covalent bond"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A covalent bond in which both electrons originated from the same atom; also called dative bond."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "3686966191",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2854315638",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " are contributed by an electron pair donor (a Lewis base) to an electron pair acceptor (a Lewis acid). In a coordination compound, also called a "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "coordination complex"
              },
              {
                "text": ", the Lewis acid is a central metal ion or atom (this is usually a transition metal or inner transition metal). The "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "central metal"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "An ion or atom to which one or more ligands is attached through coordinate covalent bonds."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "4275991860",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1018317738",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " is surrounded by Lewis bases—called "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "ligands"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A molecule or ion that bonds to the central atom of a complex ion by donating one or more electron pairs."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "427267505",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1838180053",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": "—that can be atoms, molecules, or ions. The only requirement is that they have one or more electron pairs that can be donated to the central metal."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1385435741",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Coordinate covalent bonds involve electrons from a Lewis base being donated to a metal center. The lone pairs from six water molecules form bonds to the scandium ion to form an octahedral complex."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1209612204",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "dveb0dqxfxndcoc",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "display": "block",
            "height": 197,
            "id": "2377432356",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/07/075E3648A92D3AB9B69E7F548C868EB9/coord_coval_bonds_updated.png",
            "type": "img",
            "width": 400
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "825908534",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "editor": "slate",
        "id": "1191602660",
        "textDirection": "ltr",
        "type": "content"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "As shown above, in formulas of complex ions, the bonding atom of a polyatomic ligand is often written first (for example, OH"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": " instead of H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O), and in structure drawings, the bonding atom is drawn connecting to the line representing a covalent bond in the complex. For example, M–OH"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": " is a representation that more accurately shows the shared electron bond between M and O, compared to M–H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O, since hydrogen does not donate electrons or bond to the metal."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3192753632",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "editor": "slate",
        "id": "2011065012",
        "textDirection": "ltr",
        "type": "content"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "activity_id": 78947,
            "children": [],
            "id": "2898400577",
            "type": "activity-reference"
          }
        ],
        "id": "1401327804",
        "layout": "vertical",
        "purpose": "learnbydoing",
        "type": "group"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "coordination sphere"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The central metal atom or ion plus the attached ligands of a complex."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2232951778",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "880152744",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " consists of the central metal ion or atom plus its attached ligands, and brackets in a formula or structure enclose the coordination sphere. The "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "coordination number"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The number of coordinate covalent bonds to the central metal atom in a complex or the number of closest contacts to an atom in a crystalline form."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1975302708",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3436331835",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " of the central metal ion or atom is the number of donor atoms bonded to it. Species outside the brackets are not part of the coordination sphere. These may be counterions that balance the charge of the complex ion in a coordination compound."
              }
            ],
            "id": "544063466",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Examples of complexes with coordination numbers of 2, 4 and 6 are shown in the figure below. Each of these ligands is a "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "monodentate ligand"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A ligand that attaches to a central metal through just one coordinate covalent bond."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2799481402",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2241106950",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ", from the Greek for “one toothed,” meaning the ligand connects with the central metal through only "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "one atom"
              },
              {
                "text": ". In these cases, the number of ligands and the coordination number are equal."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2083681944",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "In [Ag(NH"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "3"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": ")"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "]"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "+"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": ", two NH"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "3"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " ligands bond to the central Ag"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "+"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " ion, which has a coordination number of 2 ("
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "left"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "). In [Cu(Cl)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "4"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "]"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "−"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": ", four Cl"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "−"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " ligands bond to the central Cu"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "2+"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " ion, which has a coordination number of 4 ("
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "center"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "). In [Co(H"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "O)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "6"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "]"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "2+"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": ", six H"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "O ligands bond to the central Co"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "2+"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " ion, which has a coordination number of 6 ("
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "right"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "). The geometries of these complexes are the same as we have seen with VSEPR theory for main-group elements: linear, tetrahedral, and octahedral."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3995350960",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "g5tts7bj4za2uvy",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "display": "block",
            "id": "1807887341",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/E5/E5D1E5AA5DA02C1A7983B6B0943C8C20/coord_numbers_updated.png",
            "type": "img",
