          }
        ],
        "id": "3292170794",
        "layout": "vertical",
        "purpose": "didigetthis",
        "type": "group"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Many other ligands can coordinate to the central metal in more complex fashions. For example, ethylenediamine (H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "NCH"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "CH"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "NH"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "), often abbreviated as “en,” contains two nitrogen atoms, each of which has a lone pair that can serve as a Lewis base; see figure (a) below. Both of the nitrogen atoms can coordinate to a single metal center, so the en ligand is called a "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "bidentate ligand"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A ligand that coordinates to one central metal through coordinate bonds from two different atoms."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2871989120",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1640322719",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ", from the Greek for “two teeth,” meaning that the ligand connects with the central metal through two atoms. The complex [Co(en)"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "]"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "3+"
              },
              {
                "text": " has three bidentate en ligands, and the coordination number of the cobalt(III) ion is 6; see figure (b). The most common coordination numbers are 2, 4, and 6, but examples of all coordination numbers from 1 to 15 are known."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3738625266",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(a)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " The ethylenediamine (en) ligand contains two nitrogen atoms with lone pairs that can coordinate to the metal center. "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(b)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " The cobalt(III) complex [Co(en)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "3"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "]3+ contains three bidentate en ligands, each forming two bonds to the cobalt ion, so the complex has a coordination number of 6 and an octahedral geometry."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2636973772",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "j9u9cqb3z1kwj8e",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "display": "block",
            "height": 228.21875,
            "id": "1792016589",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/03/03950FE4DD9D8E5AA4B94387A15C157A/en_and_en_complex.png",
            "type": "img",
            "width": 551.1958111231662
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "2427152475",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "editor": "slate",
        "id": "958248690",
        "textDirection": "ltr",
        "type": "content"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Bidentate ligands usually contain at least two atoms in their chemical structure between the atoms that donate lone pairs. For example, the two carbons in the en ligand (shown above) space the donating two N atoms far enough apart that the resulting bond angles in the ligand molecule are still energetically feasible. The same is observed in the anion of the amino acid glycine for the spacing between the N and O atoms (see figure below) and in many other ligands."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3639482757",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "The anion NH"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "CH"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "CO"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "− "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "of the amino acid glycine is a bidentate ligand with both a nitrogen and an oxygen atom coordinating to the platinum(II) metal center. In this complex ion, two of these ligands attach to a Pt(II) center."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1186450312",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "d1prkiwo63h6sc4",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "display": "block",
            "id": "4035505586",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/40/40483B148E956AE748FC446A5D2DC60C/glycine_Pt.png",
            "type": "img",
            "width": 300
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "1879712284",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "editor": "slate",
        "id": "1813313306",
        "textDirection": "ltr",
        "type": "content"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Any ligand that bonds to a central metal ion by more than one donor atom is called a "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "polydentate"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A ligand that is attached to a central metal ion by bonds from two or more donor atoms, named with prefixes specifying how many donors are present (e.g., hexadentate = six coordinate bonds formed)."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2826348709",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2039659940",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " (“many teeth”) ligand because it can coordinate to (or “bite” into) the metal center with more than one bond. The prefix indicates the number of electron donor atoms in the ligand, so tridentate, tetradentate, pentadentate, and hexadentate ligands contain three, four, five, and six donor atoms, respectively. The term "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "chelate"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A complex formed from a polydentate ligand attached to a central metal."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2917321855",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1857952397",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " (pronounced “KEY-late”), from the Greek for “claw,” is also used to describe this type of interaction. Many polydentate ligands are "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "chelating ligands"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A ligand that attaches to a central metal ion by bonds from two or more donor atoms."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1513991721",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1822070285",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " (also called "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "chelating agents"
              },
              {
                "text": "), and a complex consisting of one or more of these ligands and a central metal is a chelate. A chelating ligand bonds to the central atom much like a crab’s claw would pinch a small rock (or your finger!). Ethylenediamine is one example of a chelate with polydentate ligands. The heme complex in hemoglobin (shown below) is an important biological example."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1495105327",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "The heme complex, a component of hemoglobin, contains a single tetradentate ligand (a porphyrin ring system) with four nitrogen atoms that coordinate to iron. The porphyrin ring system is an example of a chelating ligand."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1406841071",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "u701j45t6mp05fp",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "display": "block",
            "height": 285,
            "id": "2478451902",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/F9/F9CF1B9C6CA3AA98706BB1DABF4ECBD3/porphyrin.jpeg",
            "type": "img",
            "width": 400
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "3470172898",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
