      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Polydentate ligands may have physical constraints on which atoms can donate a lone pair and how many connections can be made to a single metal atom. It is useful to know (or recall) that single bonds can usually rotate freely, while double bonds, systems of alternating double and single bonds, and bonds in rings cannot rotate without enough energy to break some chemical bonds."
              }
            ],
            "id": "741648247",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "editor": "slate",
        "id": "3504374148",
        "textDirection": "ltr",
        "type": "content"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "activity_id": 79047,
            "children": [],
            "id": "4166865833",
            "type": "activity-reference"
          }
        ],
        "id": "2612755653",
        "layout": "vertical",
        "purpose": "learnbydoing",
        "type": "group"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "activity_id": 80462,
            "children": [],
            "id": "1778410951",
            "type": "activity-reference"
          }
        ],
        "id": "543105384",
        "layout": "vertical",
        "purpose": "didigetthis",
        "type": "group"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Once the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "denticity"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The number of donor atoms in a ligand that directly bind to a central metal atom or ion in a coordination complex. It indicates how many points of attachment the ligand has to the metal (monodentate, bidentate, etc.) based on the number of coordinating atoms."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "676094401",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1549687898",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " of the ligands is determined, we can predict the coordination number and ideal geometry of transition metal complexes based on a chemical formula. Ligand denticity can be determined by inspection (as you have just practiced), and it is useful to know the denticity of some common ligands."
              }
            ],
            "id": "640258562",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "editor": "slate",
        "id": "2911726432",
        "textDirection": "ltr",
        "type": "content"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Identify the coordination number for each complex."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2370023437",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "editor": "slate",
